Chapter 1 General Provisions

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on Fostering and Supporting Village Enterprises and matters necessary for its implementation.

Article 2 (Definitions)
In this Decree, "social and solidarity economy organization" means any of the following organizations:
1. A village enterprise;
2. A social enterprise under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Social Enterprise Promotion Act;
3. A cooperative, federation of cooperatives, social cooperative, federation of social cooperatives, or federation of different types of cooperatives under Article 2 of the Framework Act on Cooperatives;
4. A self-support enterprise under Article 18 of the National Basic Living Security Act;
5. A social venture enterprise under Article 2 (10) of the Special Act on the Promotion of Venture Businesses.

Article 3 (Criteria for Village Residents)
(1) A village resident under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act on Fostering and Supporting Village Enterprises (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") shall be a person falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. A person whose resident registration under Article 6 of the Resident Registration Act is in the Eup, Myeon, or Dong in which the village enterprise is located and operates;
2. A person who works at a workplace located in the Eup, Myeon, or Dong in which the village enterprise is located and operates, or who operates a place of business in that Eup, Myeon, or Dong.
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), when designating a village enterprise pursuant to Article 11 of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may use a Si, Gun, or Gu (meaning an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply) as the geographical basis if the Minister deems it necessary, taking into consideration the purpose of establishing the village enterprise, the residents' daily living areas, etc.

Chapter 2 Framework for Fostering and Supporting Village Enterprises

Article 4 (Establishment of Master Plans, etc.)
(1) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety intends to establish or modify a master plan for fostering and supporting village enterprises under Article 6 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "master plan"), the Minister shall do so following deliberation by the Central Committee for the Fostering and Support of Village Enterprises under Article 9 of the Act: Provided, That deliberation shall not be required in any of the following cases:
1. Where reflecting matters changed by an amendment of the Act or the enactment, amendment, or repeal of other statutes and regulations;
2. Where correcting mistakes, typographical errors, omissions, or other comparable obvious errors;
3. Where modifying minor matters that do not affect the basic direction, principal objectives, and tasks to be pursued under the master plan.
(2) Where necessary to establish or modify a master plan, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies or heads of local governments to submit opinions or related materials. In such cases, the heads of relevant agencies requested to submit opinions or materials shall comply unless there is a special reason not to do so.
(3) When the Minister of the Interior and Safety establishes or modifies a master plan, the Minister shall make its contents public through the website of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety, etc.

Article 5 (Subjects, Scope, and Methods of Fact-Finding Surveys, etc.)
(1) A fact-finding survey under Article 8 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "fact-finding survey") shall cover all village enterprises.
(2) A fact-finding survey shall include the following matters:
1. The general status of village enterprises;
2. Business performance, including sales and employment;
3. Records of social contribution activities;
4. Checking and management of inventory registers;
5. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of the Interior and Safety or the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, an Integrated Special City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as "Mayors/Do Governors").
(3) Fact-finding surveys may be conducted by means of written surveys, interviews, on-site surveys, etc.
(4) Where necessary to conduct fact-finding surveys efficiently, the Minister of the Interior and Safety and Mayors/Do Governors may share information on and consult about the items, timing, methods, etc. of fact-finding surveys.
(5) The Minister of the Interior and Safety and Mayors/Do Governors shall endeavor to reflect the results of fact-finding surveys in the following matters:
1. The Minister of the Interior and Safety: Establishment and improvement of master plans and policies to support village enterprises;
2. Mayors/Do Governors: Establishment and improvement of implementation plans under Article 7 (1) of the Act and policies to support village enterprises within their jurisdictional areas.

Article 6 (Composition of the Central Committee for the Fostering and Support of Village Enterprises, etc.)
(1) The Central Committee for the Fostering and Support of Village Enterprises under Article 9 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Central Committee") shall consist of not more than 16 members, including one chairperson.
(2) The chairperson shall be elected by and from among the members under paragraph (3) 1 through 5 (hereinafter referred to as "commissioned members").
(3) Members of the Central Committee shall be commissioned or appointed by the Minister of the Interior and Safety from among the following persons, taking gender into consideration. In such cases, commissioned members shall constitute a majority of the prescribed number of members.
1. Persons representing village enterprises;
2. Executives and employees of village enterprise support institutions under Article 16 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "support institutions");
3. Persons representing social and solidarity economy organizations;
4. Executives and employees of institutions and organizations that support social and solidarity economy organizations;
5. Experts with extensive knowledge and field experience concerning village enterprises or social and solidarity economy organizations;
6. Public officials of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety responsible for village enterprise affairs.
(4) The term of office of commissioned members shall be two years, and they may be reappointed consecutively only once.
(5) The Central Committee shall have one secretary to handle its affairs, and the secretary shall be designated by the Minister of the Interior and Safety from among public officials of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

Article 7 (Removal of Members)
The Minister of the Interior and Safety may remove a commissioned member if the member falls under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Where the member is deemed to have difficulty performing duties over a prolonged period due to physical or mental infirmity, etc.;
2. Where the member has engaged in misconduct related to duties;
3. Where the member is deemed unsuitable to serve as a member due to neglect of duties, impairment of dignity, or any other reason;
4. Where the member fails to recuse himself or herself pursuant to Article 8 (3), despite falling under a ground for disqualification under any subparagraph of Article 8 (1) or considering that there are circumstances making it difficult to expect fairness in deliberation from himself or herself;
5. Where the member divulges confidential information learned in connection with duties or uses it for personal purposes;
6. Where the member voluntarily expresses a wish to be removed or indicates that it is difficult to perform duties.

Article 8 (Disqualification, Challenge, and Recusal of Members)
(1) A member of the Central Committee who falls under any of the following subparagraphs shall be disqualified from the deliberation and resolution of the Central Committee:
1. Where the member of the Central Committee or the member's spouse or former spouse is a party to the matter concerned (including an executive of the party where the party is a corporation, organization, etc.; hereinafter the same shall apply), or is a joint holder of rights or a joint obligor with a party to the matter;
2. Where the member of the Central Committee is or was a relative of a party to the matter concerned;
3. Where the member of the Central Committee is performing or has performed advisory work, research, services (including subcontracted services), appraisals, or investigations regarding the matter concerned;
4. Where the member of the Central Committee or a corporation, organization, etc. to which the member belongs is or was an agent of a party to the matter concerned.
(2) Where a member of the Central Committee has a ground for disqualification under any subparagraph of paragraph (1), or circumstances make it difficult to expect fair deliberation and resolution from the member, a party to the matter may file a challenge against the member with the Central Committee, and the Central Committee shall decide by resolution whether to accept the challenge. In such cases, the member subject to the challenge shall not participate in that resolution.
(3) A member of the Central Committee shall voluntarily recuse himself or herself from the deliberation and resolution on a matter if the member falls under a ground for disqualification under any subparagraph of paragraph (1) or considers that there are circumstances making it difficult to expect fairness in deliberation from himself or herself.

Article 9 (Operation of the Central Committee)
(1) The chairperson shall convene meetings of the Central Committee and preside over them.
(2) A meeting of the Central Committee shall be opened with the attendance of a majority of its incumbent members, and resolutions shall be passed with the affirmative vote of a majority of the members present.
(3) Where the chairperson of the Central Committee deems it necessary for the efficient operation of the Central Committee, the chairperson may request relevant public officials, representatives of corporations applying for designation as a village enterprise or for eligibility for support, or persons providing relevant information to attend a meeting of the Central Committee and state their opinions.

Article 10 (Subcommittees)
(1) The Central Committee may establish a subcommittee to deliberate on matters concerning the designation of village enterprises under Article 11 of the Act or the selection of village enterprises eligible for support under Article 12 (1) of the Act.
(2) A subcommittee shall consist of at least six and no more than eight members of the Central Committee.
(3) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of subcommittees, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be determined by the chairperson following a resolution of the Central Committee.

Article 11 (Substitute Committee)
(1) Pursuant to the proviso of Article 9 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may designate a committee meeting all the following requirements as a substitute committee to perform the functions of the Central Committee:
1. It shall deliberate on matters related to the matters for deliberation set out in the subparagraphs of Article 9 (2) of the Act;
2. A majority of its members shall be nongovernmental members with expertise in relevant fields, such as village enterprises, social and solidarity economy organizations, local communities, local autonomy, accounting, and law;
3. It shall have standards for preventing conflicts of interest, including disqualification, challenge, and recusal of members.
(2) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety designates a substitute committee pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister shall publicly announce that fact through the website of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety or other means.
(3) A substitute committee designated pursuant to paragraph (1) may establish subcommittees under Article 10.

Article 12 (Matters for Deliberation by the Central Committee)
The term "matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 9 (2) 6 of the Act means matters concerning the selection of village enterprises to receive support under Article 12 (1) of the Act (limited to cases where the Minister of the Interior and Safety provides the support).

Article 13 (Composition and Operation of City/Do Committees for Fostering and Supporting Village Enterprises)
(1) Where Mayors/Do Governors establish City/Do Committees for Fostering and Supporting Village Enterprises (hereinafter referred to as "City/Do Committees") pursuant to the main clause of Article 10 (1) of the Act, matters necessary for the composition and operation of the City/Do Committees shall be prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government, including the following matters:
1. Nongovernmental members with expertise in relevant fields, such as village enterprises, social and solidarity economy organizations, local communities, local autonomy, accounting, and law, shall constitute a majority of the prescribed membership;
2. There shall be operating rules covering matters such as the convening of meetings, quorums for proceedings and resolutions, and secretaries;
3. There shall be standards for preventing conflicts of interest, including disqualification, challenge, and recusal of members.
(2) The term "conditions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso of Article 10 (1) of the Act means the following conditions:
1. The organization shall deliberate on matters related to the matters for deliberation by City/Do Committees under the main clause of Article 10 (1) of the Act;
2. The organization shall meet the requirements in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1).
(3) Matters necessary for the designation and operation of organizations under the proviso of Article 10 (1) of the Act may be prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government.

CHAPTER III DESIGNATION AND SUPPORT OF VILLAGE ENTERPRISES

Article 14 (Requirements for Designation as a Village Enterprise)
(1) The term "number of capital contributors, capital contribution ratios, and capital contribution ratios among specially related persons prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 11 (1) 2 of the Act means the following requirements:
1. At least five village residents shall have made capital contributions to the village enterprise;
2. Village residents shall constitute at least 70 percent of the persons who have made capital contributions to the village enterprise: Provided, That in cases falling under the following items, the proportion shall be at least the ratio specified in the relevant item:
(a) Cases under Article 3 (2) (excluding cases where the relevant Si/Gun/Gu is a depopulation area under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Special Act on Support for Depopulation Areas): 80 percent;
(b) Cases falling under Article 12 (3) of the Act and Article 17 of this Decree: 50 percent;
3. The capital contribution ratio of any one capital contributor shall not exceed 30 percent;
4. The total capital contributions of a capital contributor and specially related persons (meaning the capital contributor's spouse, lineal ascendants and descendants, and siblings) shall not exceed 50 percent of the total capital contributions.
(2) The term "requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 11 (1) 5 of the Act means the following requirements. In such cases, detailed criteria shall be prescribed by the Minister of the Interior and Safety.
1. It shall have a community character, such as village residents participating directly in decision-making and profits being returned to residents;
2. It shall have a public character, such as contributing to the local community and realizing social value;
3. It shall have a local character, such as using local resources and contributing to the local economy;
4. It shall have the characteristics of a business, such as the ability actually to conduct business activities, the ability to generate revenue, and sustainability.

Article 15 (Procedure for Designation as a Village Enterprise)
(1) To designate village enterprises pursuant to Article 11 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall publicly announce annually the matters necessary for designation as a village enterprise, such as designation criteria, application methods, and accompanying documents.
(2) A corporation seeking designation as a village enterprise shall submit an application and relevant documents to the Minister of the Interior and Safety in accordance with the public announcement under paragraph (1).
(3) Upon receipt of an application under paragraph (2), the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall review and verify whether the requirements for designation as a village enterprise are met, the current status of the village enterprise, etc., and designate the village enterprise following deliberation by the Central Committee.
(4) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety designates a village enterprise, the Minister shall notify the relevant village enterprise and Mayors/Do Governors, and publicly announce the designation through the website of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety or other means.

Article 16 (Detailed Items of Support for Village Enterprises)
(1) The term "matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 12 (1) 5 of the Act means the following support:
1. Support for sales channels and promotion of products and services;
2. Support for upgrading products and services and for quality control;
3. Support necessary for operating village enterprises, such as personnel costs and operating expenses;
4. Other support deemed necessary by the Minister of the Interior and Safety and the heads of local governments.
(2) Article 15 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the procedures for selecting village enterprises to receive support under Article 12 (1) of the Act (excluding deliberation by the Central Committee under paragraph (3) of that Article where a local government provides the support). In such cases, "designation" shall be construed as "support," "Minister of the Interior and Safety" as "Minister of the Interior and Safety and the heads of local governments," "Article 11 (1) of the Act" as "Article 12 (1) of the Act," "annually" as "where there is a support plan," and "Ministry of the Interior and Safety" as "Ministry of the Interior and Safety and local governments."

Article 17 (Standards for the Proportion of Youth Members)
The term "ratio prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 12 (3) of the Act means the ratio specified in the following classifications:
1. Where the proportion of youth (meaning youth under the main clause of subparagraph 1 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Youth; hereinafter the same shall apply) in the population of the Si/Gun/Gu in which the village enterprise is located is less than 15 percent: 30 percent;
2. Where the proportion of youth in the population of the Si/Gun/Gu in which the village enterprise is located is at least 15 percent and no more than 20 percent: 40 percent;
3. Where the proportion of youth in the population of the Si/Gun/Gu in which the village enterprise is located exceeds 20 percent: 50 percent.

Article 18 (Procedures for Revocation of Designation as a Village Enterprise)
(1) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems that a village enterprise falls under any subparagraph of Article 13 (1) of the Act, the Minister shall conduct a fact-finding investigation or take other such action. In such cases, where necessary to verify the facts investigated, etc., the Minister may hear the opinions of the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (in the case of the head of a Gu, meaning the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply), a support institution, or external experts.
(2) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall decide whether to revoke the designation after reviewing the results of the fact-finding investigation under paragraph (1), the opinions of the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, support institutions, etc., the results of the hearing under Article 13 (2) of the Act, and other relevant matters. In such cases, the Minister shall notify the relevant village enterprise, the relevant Mayors/Do Governors, and the heads of Si/Gun/Gu of the result.
(3) A village enterprise notified of revocation of its designation pursuant to the latter part of paragraph (2) shall submit a performance report to the Minister of the Interior and Safety within 14 days from the date of receipt of the notification.
(4) Upon receipt of a performance report under paragraph (3), the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall take measures such as requiring the return of subsidies under the Subsidy Management Act.
(5) Where a village enterprise considers it difficult to continue its business, it may request the Minister of the Interior and Safety to revoke its designation. In such cases, the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall verify the intentions of the capital contributors to the village enterprise, etc., and, where the designation is revoked, shall notify the relevant village enterprise, the relevant Mayors/Do Governors, and the heads of Si/Gun/Gu of the result.
(6) Paragraphs (3) and (4) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the submission of a performance report by a village enterprise notified of revocation of its designation pursuant to the latter part of paragraph (5) and to measures such as the return of subsidies.

CHAPTER IV ESTABLISHMENT OF A FOUNDATION FOR FOSTERING VILLAGE ENTERPRISES

Article 19 (Village Enterprise Day)
"Day prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 14 (1) of the Act means April 2 each year.

Article 20 (Establishment and Operation of a Village Enterprise Information System)
(1) Through the village enterprise information system under Article 15 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may manage information on the operating status of village enterprises, including their sales, numbers of workers by sex and age, and social contribution activities.
(2) For supporting the establishment and operation of village enterprises, identifying actual conditions, and implementing policies, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may have the heads of local governments and support institutions use the village enterprise information system under Article 15 (1) of the Act.

Article 21 (Designation of Village Enterprise Support Institutions)
(1) Pursuant to Article 16 (1) of the Act, Mayors/Do Governors may designate as support institutions institutions with the professional personnel, facilities, experience in performing affairs, operating capabilities, and other resources necessary to perform village enterprise support affairs.
(2) When designating a support institution pursuant to paragraph (1), a Mayor/Do Governor shall prescribe matters concerning the scope of its affairs, the designation period, reporting of operating performance, guidance and supervision, and other matters.
(3) Mayors/Do Governors shall endeavor to ensure stable operation of support institutions, taking into account the securing of professional personnel and continuity of support affairs.
(4) Detailed matters necessary for the establishment, designation and operation of support institutions, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government.

CHAPTER V SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 22 (Delegation of Authority)
(1) Pursuant to Article 20 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall delegate the following authority to Mayors/Do Governors:
1. The following matters among the authority concerning designation of village enterprises under Article 11 of the Act (including cases where the relevant provisions apply mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 16 (2)):
(a) Public announcements concerning designation of village enterprises under Article 15 (1);
(b) Review of whether the designation requirements under Article 14 (2) have been satisfied, among the matters for review under Article 15 (3);
2. Matters concerning supervision of village enterprises under Article 19 (1) of the Act.
(2) Pursuant to Article 20 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall delegate the following authority to the heads of Sis/Guns/Gus:
1. The following matters among the authority concerning designation of village enterprises under Article 11 of the Act (including cases where the relevant provisions apply mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 16 (2)):
(a) Receipt of applications for designation of village enterprises and related documents under Article 15 (2);
(b) Review of whether the designation requirements under Article 11 (1) 1 through 4 of the Act have been satisfied, pursuant to Article 15 (3);
(c) On-site verification of the status of village enterprises under Article 15 (3);
(d) Notification of designation to the relevant village enterprise under Article 15 (4);
2. The following matters among the authority concerning revocation of designation of village enterprises under Article 13 of the Act:
(a) Hearings concerning revocation of designation of village enterprises under Article 13 (2) of the Act;
(b) Investigation of facts concerning grounds for revocation of designation under the former part of Article 18 (1);
(c) Notification to village enterprises of whether their designation is revoked, under the latter part of Article 18 (2);
(d) Receipt of performance reports and measures for return of subsidies and other matters under Article 18 (3) and (4) (including cases where those provisions apply mutatis mutandis pursuant to paragraph (6) of that Article);
(e) Receipt of requests for revocation of designation under the former part of Article 18 (5);
(f) Verification of the intentions of contributors to village enterprises and other matters, and notification of revocation of designation, under the latter part of Article 18 (5);
3. Inspections to verify whether village enterprises satisfy the requirements for designation under Article 19 (1) of the Act;
4. Imposition and collection of administrative fines under Article 22 of the Act.
(3) The heads of Sis/Guns/Gus shall submit the matters handled pursuant to paragraph (2) to the Mayors/Do Governors, who shall compile the matters they have handled pursuant to paragraph (1) and the matters submitted by the heads of Sis/Guns/Gus and submit them to the Minister of the Interior and Safety.

Article 23 (Entrustment of Affairs)
(1) Pursuant to Article 20 (2) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may entrust affairs under subparagraphs 1 and 2 of that paragraph and paragraph (2) of this Article to institutions, corporations, or organizations that possess specialized capabilities and perform functions concerning village enterprises.
(2) "Affairs prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 20 (2) 3 of the Act means affairs concerning establishment, operation and maintenance of the village enterprise information system under Article 15 (1) of the Act.
(3) When entrusting affairs pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall publicly notify the entrusted institution, corporation or organization and the details of the entrusted affairs.
(4) The head of a local government may entrust affairs of the relevant local government under Article 20 (2) of the Act, as prescribed by ordinance.

Article 24 (Formulation and Notification of Operating Guidelines)
The Minister of the Interior and Safety may prescribe operating guidelines concerning detailed procedures necessary for implementing the Act and this Decree, evaluation and performance management, execution and settlement of support projects, recovery of assistance, and other matters, and notify Mayors/Do Governors and heads of Sis/Guns/Gus thereof.

Article 25 (Criteria for Imposition of Administrative Fines)
The criteria for imposing administrative fines under Article 22 (3) of the Act shall be as specified in the Annex.

ADDENDA <Presidential Decree No. 36568, August 11, 2026>

This Decree shall enter into force on August 15, 2026.
